{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/proof-audit-compile-correctness/SKILL.md && printf '\\\\n--- review-area conventions ---\\\\n' && find history/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses -maxdepth 1 -type f -name 'FF*-*review*.md' -printf '%f\\\\n' | sort | tail -n 12\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: proof-audit-compile-correctness\ndescription: Audit mathematical proof files and derivations for logical correctness while separating TeX/Lean/build success from truth. Use when the user asks to check a named proof file, verify whether a proof is correct, red-team a theorem note, inspect circularity, compare compile success to mathematical validity, or prepare closed-book proof-agent prompts.\n---\n\n# Proof Audit Compile Correctness\n\nAudit the exact artifact named by the user. Do not treat a compiled PDF as evidence that the proof is correct.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the named proof file and nearby status/context files.\n2. Compile or build if relevant, and record build status separately.\n3. Identify the theorem statement, assumptions, definitions, and claimed route.\n4. Check each load-bearing step for:\n   - hidden assumptions;\n   - circular use of the target theorem;\n   - unjustified reductions;\n   - domain/codomain mismatches;\n   - convention changes;\n   - numerical evidence promoted to proof;\n   - stale status claims contradicted by later audits.\n5. If a proof fails, identify the first fatal gap, not just a list of concerns.\n6. If useful, create or suggest a minimal next artifact: lemma statement, counterexample search, standalone TeX extraction, Lean target, or closed-book proof prompt.\n\n## Status Labels\n\nUse direct labels:\n\n- `compiled_not_checked`;\n- `compiled_but_incorrect`;\n- `proof_gap_found`;\n- `numerical_evidence_only`;\n- `candidate_proof_needs_review`;\n- `approved_with_residual_risks`;\n- `proved` only when the argument survives the audit.\n\n## Closed-Book Proof Prompts\n\nWhen preparing prompts for outside proof agents, do not mention that a problem is open unless the user asks. Emphasize:\n\n- no internet/literature lookup;\n- solve from the statement;\n- avoid circularity and stronger false lemmas;\n- report failed approaches explicitly;\n- provide exact proof or exact counterexample.\n\n## Final Report\n\nLead with findings ordered by severity. Include file/location references, build status, correctness verdict, and the next concrete proof task.\n\n--- review-area conventions ---\nFF11-integration-adversarial-review.md\nFF11-prompt-adversarial-review.md\nFF12-integration-adversarial-review.md\nFF12-prompt-adversarial-review.md\nFF15-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF16-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF17-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF18-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF19-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF20-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF21-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\nFF22-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
